artifact.self_contained_problem:
In applied information geometry, a data object (a "complex system" or "Big Data" set) is typically modeled by a (possibly singular) statistical manifold: a measurable family of probability distributions $\{p_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}$ on some sample space, together with the Fisher information metric $g_F$ and a dually flat pair of affine connections $(\nabla^{(\alpha)}, \nabla^{(-\alpha)})$ arising from the Amari $\alpha$-family, when these structures exist. By a "prescribed geometric structure" we mean a specified tuple such as a Riemannian metric, a Hessian (dually flat) structure, a Kähler structure, a contact or symplectic structure, or a foliation of prescribed codimension and holonomy. By a "prescribed topological structure" we mean a specified homotopy/homeomorphism/diffeomorphism type, a prescribed CW or simplicial decomposition, a prescribed sheaf-cohomological invariant, or a prescribed (persistent) homology profile.

Problem. Develop effective criteria, and where possible an algorithm, that take as input a complex system or data object $X$ (presented either as an empirical distribution, a parameterized statistical model, or a finite metric/measure space) and a prescribed geometric structure $G$ or prescribed topological structure $T$, and decide:

(a) whether $X$ admits a representation as a statistical manifold (or stratified statistical space) carrying the structure $G$;

(b) whether the underlying space of $X$ admits the topological structure $T$;

(c) when such structures exist, classify them up to the natural equivalence (isometry, affine equivalence, diffeomorphism, homotopy equivalence).

In particular, identify obstructions (curvature, characteristic classes, cohomological invariants, entropy/divergence-based invariants) whose vanishing is necessary and sufficient for the existence of $G$ or $T$ on $X$.

